      "text": "To find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start with the given temperature equation:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to solve this equation for \\( t \\). Start by simplifying the equation:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nSubtract 77 from both sides to set the equation to zero:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nMultiply the entire equation by -1 to make it easier to work with:\n\n\\[\nT^2 - 12T + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( T = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\):\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThe solutions to the quadratic equation are \\( T = 9 \\) and \\( T = 3 \\). The larger \\( T \\) value represents the larger time past noon when the temperature was exactly 77 degrees. Therefore, the largest \\( t \\) value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
